---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Wordmark from '../components/Wordmark.astro';
import FootnoteRef from '../components/FootnoteRef.astro';
import Footnotes from '../components/Footnotes.astro';
import { createFootnotes } from '../lib/footnotes.js';

// Notes in order of appearance; markers (*, **, …) are derived from position.
const notes = createFootnotes([
  `I use Claude for building it. I'll share my process as openly as I can, but
   I want to be honest about what that actually means right now: Claude is a
   powerful accelerator if you already know how to build things, not yet a tool
   that hands that ability to someone starting from zero. It also means that building is fast, and hence it's tempting to build unnecessary things, instead of living.`,
]);
---

<BaseLayout title="About — Short Obsessions">
  <Nav current="about" />

  <main class="about">
    <Wordmark href="/" dock />
    <div class="about__inner">
      <article class="about__prose">
      <h1 class="sr-only">About</h1>

      <p>
        I created <i class="about__name">short obsessions</i> because I had a
        thousand ideas but they rarely became anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's not a blog, nor it is a shop. It's a collection of rabbit holes where you will learn, have fun and
        perhaps shop. But do not expect typical "Collections", "About", "Blog"
        sections. Expect to be confused...
      </p>
      <p>
        You're always welcome to reach out to me with: "The video on your main
        page is driving me crazy", "ooo... I didn't know about the Indie Web.
        Thanks!". Or anything else... how you collaborate with the website will
        ultimately drive what the website becomes.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a place built for exploration. As today's web is created more
        with AI which uses "templates" and "safe" decisions, here I harness the
        power of AI<FootnoteRef note={notes[0]} /> as a way to accelerate
        deployment of ideas before they become unexciting in my mind.
      </p>
      

      <section class="about__section">
        <h2 class="about__h2">Experiments</h2>
      <p>
        I have a naive idea that in the age of AI agents scanning the internet,
        authenticity and "human creativity" might actually be able to surface
        above the companies which spend millions feeding us generic advertising.
        At least for a little while.. perhaps..
      </p>
      <p>
        Since agents are using the same search engines as us, but at the same
        time have the capacity to open every link to see if it answers our
        questions, perhaps the "weird", "different" places will surface? The traditional SEO meta tags will no longer be sufficient when "clicking through" is done by bots.
      </p>
      <p>
        The alternative is unfortunately what I call the "shallow web". That is,
        an internet where great things exist but we are unable to find them. If
        you are curious about "the other", wholesome internet, I suggest starting
        at <a href="https://www.marginalia-search.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">www.marginalia-search.com</a>
      </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about__section">
        <h2 class="about__h2">Design</h2>
      <p>
        I don't have the courage to call myself a designer. In fact,
        <i class="about__name">short obsessions</i>' main goal is for me to
        release things before the self-doubt
        kills them. At the same time, I am sad that the free, open source
        internet does not care enough about design or about "non-technical"
        people. There exist countless free tools, resources, and software that
        are unfortunately inaccessible to anyone who is not "a programmer" or who
        is allergic to the aesthetics of Windows 98. Hence, wherever possible, I
        will try to share what I have learned in my countless "internet
        adventures".
      </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about__section">
        <h2 class="about__h2">Everything is because of something</h2>
      <p>
        Creating <i class="about__name">short obsessions</i> is a personal journey across the "small web".
        Every decision (e.g. where to host the website, what font to use, what
        colours) is backed by an internet research adventure. Unfortunately, this
        means that the website becomes what it is very slowly. But perhaps that,
        in itself, is not the point.
      </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about__section">
        <h2 class="about__h2">Some questions I want to explore</h2>
      <ul class="about__questions">
        <li>
          Can we create websites that are "discovered" without compromising
          authenticity?
        </li>
        <li>What if shops were about learning?</li>
        <li>
          What could a future online shop look like when "building" is cheap?
          Perhaps we can free ourselves from "templates" and "categories".
        </li>
      </ul>
      </section>

      <Footnotes notes={notes} />

      <footer class="about__footer">
        <p class="about__signoff">so..| Ela</p>
      </footer>
      </article>

      <a href="/" class="about__back">&larr; Back</a>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    min-height: 100vh;
    /* Top clears the docking wordmark (fixed at --page-pad-y, ~48px tall) plus a
       masthead gap, so the first paragraph sits just below it; the mark is out
       of flow, so this padding is what reserves the space. */
    padding: calc(var(--page-pad-y) + 48px + var(--s-7)) var(--page-pad-x) calc(var(--page-pad-y) * 2);
  }

  /* Measure lives on the inner column, not the padded box — otherwise the 8vw
     gutter is subtracted *inside* the cap (border-box) and the line length
     both shrinks on wide screens and swings with viewport width. The wrapper
     holds the prose and the back-link so they share the same left edge. */
  .about__inner {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ── Type scale (Jost) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     28  page title   (Medium, lowercase — the one display step)
     18  body / questions / footnotes
     15  section label (Medium, uppercase, tracked — the "kicker" device)
     13  meta          (credit, sign-off)
     11  back link     (nav idiom)
     Hierarchy comes from size + weight + space, never from greying text
     (Müller-Brockmann). Ink for all structure & content; the dim tone is
     reserved for genuinely secondary/meta text. ──────────────────────── */

  /* Page title — the single display step. Lowercase (brand) and Medium for
     presence; generous space below seats it above the reading column. */
  /* Section markers — tracked small-caps kicker (same idiom as the nav),
     full ink + Medium weight, NOT greyed. Space ABOVE (64) far exceeds space
     BELOW (16) so each heading binds to the text it introduces (proximity). */
  .about__h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--nav-tracking);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg);
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
  }

  /* Body rhythm — paragraph gap ≈ 0.8× the ~31px leading, so blocks separate
     cleanly without breaking the vertical column (active whitespace, Ruder). */
  .about__prose p {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-5);
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* The name set off in body text — italic, so it reads as a title/name
     rather than the common phrase. Same font as the wordmark by design. */
  .about__name {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .about__prose a {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .about__prose a:hover {
    border-bottom-color: var(--fg);
  }

  .about__questions {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-4);
  }

  .about__questions li {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: var(--s-4);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
  }

  /* Sign-off + colophon credit — quiet meta in the dim tone, set apart from
     the body but sharing the same left edge. */
  .about__signoff {
    margin-top: var(--s-7);
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .about__credit {
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .about__back {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--nav-size);
    letter-spacing: var(--nav-tracking);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .about__back:hover {
    opacity: 1;
  }
</style>
